1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,624
What are Git hooks?

2
00:00:07,167 --> 00:00:11,308
Hooks are scripts Git runs automatically at certain moments:

3
00:00:11,308 --> 00:00:16,831
before a commit, after a checkout, before a push, when a server receives a push.

4
00:00:16,831 --> 00:00:19,523
They let you automate checks and tasks.

5
00:00:19,523 --> 00:00:26,426
We look at where they live, write a first one, and see the most common reason a hook "does nothing".

6
00:00:28,167 --> 00:00:32,566
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

7
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:37,253
Every new repository comes with sample hooks (disabled:

8
00:00:37,253 --> 00:00:38,838
they end in .sample):

9
00:00:38,838 --> 00:00:39,820
A first hook:

10
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:43,519
after every commit, append a line to a local log.

11
00:00:44,067 --> 00:00:45,146
A first hook:

12
00:00:45,146 --> 00:00:49,216
after every commit, append a line to a local log.

13
00:00:49,767 --> 00:00:51,366
Let's run it.

14
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:54,475
Let me try something.

15
00:00:54,475 --> 00:00:59,646
A colleague writes a pre-commit hook that should block commits containing TODO,

16
00:00:59,646 --> 00:01:02,265
saving it as a nicely named script file:

17
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,862
The commit went through:

18
00:01:04,862 --> 00:01:06,494
the hook never ran.

19
00:01:06,494 --> 00:01:13,022
Git looks for a file named exactly pre-commit; pre-commit.sh is just a file.

20
00:01:13,022 --> 00:01:13,709
Ask Git:

21
00:01:15,467 --> 00:01:19,761
Rename it, undo the bad commit, and try again:

22
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:22,213
Got it.

23
00:01:22,213 --> 00:01:24,965
What do I need to remember?

24
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:32,975
Hooks are scripts in .git/hooks/ named after an event; non-zero exit stops pre-* operations.

25
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:37,281
Exact name, executable, with a #!

26
00:01:37,281 --> 00:01:41,143
line; test with git hook run NAME.

27
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:44,059
Hooks are local and not cloned:

28
00:01:44,059 --> 00:01:46,800
CI must enforce what really matters.

29
00:01:48,533 --> 00:01:51,708
I keep hearing about pre-commit hook.

30
00:01:51,708 --> 00:01:53,338
What is it, really?

31
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:59,687
A practical pre-commit hook that checks the staged changes before every commit:

32
00:01:59,687 --> 00:02:04,522
it blocks AWS-style access keys, private keys and files over 1 MB.

33
00:02:04,522 --> 00:02:09,065
And why a local hook is a convenience, not a security control.

34
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:15,199
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

35
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:17,053
The hook:

36
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:19,880
A normal change passes:

37
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:23,827
A configuration file with a credential does not.

38
00:02:23,827 --> 00:02:32,028
(The key is AWS's documentation example, built in two parts so this course itself never contains the full pattern.).

39
00:02:33,767 --> 00:02:35,703
Let me try something.

40
00:02:35,703 --> 00:02:39,206
In a hurry, someone bypasses the hook:

41
00:02:39,767 --> 00:02:46,131
--no-verify skips pre-commit entirely, and so does a fresh clone (hooks are not cloned),

42
00:02:46,131 --> 00:02:51,411
a GUI client configured differently, or a commit made on another machine.

43
00:02:51,411 --> 00:02:53,581
The secret is now in a commit:

44
00:02:55,333 --> 00:02:56,469
Not pushed yet:

45
00:02:56,469 --> 00:03:01,994
remove the file from the commit and from Git's tracking, keep it ignored.

46
00:03:01,994 --> 00:03:07,142
(If it had been pushed, the key must be revoked and history cleaned:

47
00:03:07,142 --> 00:03:08,353
lessons 89–90.).

48
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:10,813
Got it.

49
00:03:10,813 --> 00:03:13,565
What do I need to remember?

50
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:18,650
pre-commit checks git diff --cached; exit 1 aborts the commit.

51
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:24,935
Block secrets, private keys and large files early, with clear messages.

52
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:27,416
Local hooks can be skipped:

53
00:03:27,416 --> 00:03:31,320
enforce the same checks in CI and with push protection.

54
00:03:33,067 --> 00:03:35,726
How does commit message hook work?

55
00:03:36,267 --> 00:03:42,480
The commit-msg hook receives the path of the file holding the commit message and can reject it.

56
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:45,685
We enforce the Conventional Commits format (feat:

57
00:03:45,685 --> 00:03:46,601
…, fix(scope):

58
00:03:46,601 --> 00:03:50,721
…), which tools use to generate changelogs and version numbers.

59
00:03:52,467 --> 00:03:56,866
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

60
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,250
A valid message:

61
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,650
A valid message:

62
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:05,424
Let me try something.

63
00:04:05,967 --> 00:04:07,553
The hook printed why:

64
00:04:07,553 --> 00:04:08,686
no type prefix.

65
00:04:08,686 --> 00:04:12,237
The commit was not created and nothing is lost:

66
00:04:12,237 --> 00:04:17,071
the change is still staged and modified, ready for a second try.

67
00:04:18,833 --> 00:04:20,633
Here is the fix.

68
00:04:22,367 --> 00:04:23,080
Got it.

69
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:25,831
What do I need to remember?

70
00:04:26,367 --> 00:04:31,776
commit-msg gets the message file as $1; exit 1 rejects the commit.

71
00:04:32,333 --> 00:04:33,954
Conventional Commits:

72
00:04:33,954 --> 00:04:34,880
type(scope):

73
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:37,813
description, machine-readable history.

74
00:04:38,367 --> 00:04:42,471
Enforce it in CI as well; hooks can be skipped.

75
00:04:44,233 --> 00:04:46,908
Why would I need Git hooks in teams?

76
00:04:47,467 --> 00:04:50,201
Hooks in .git/hooks/ belong to one clone:

77
00:04:50,201 --> 00:04:53,069
they are never committed, cloned or pushed.

78
00:04:53,069 --> 00:04:59,672
Teams share hooks by committing them to a normal folder and pointing Git at it with core.hooksPath,

79
00:04:59,672 --> 00:05:05,541
and they enforce the important checks in CI, because local hooks are optional by nature.

80
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:11,700
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

81
00:05:12,233 --> 00:05:15,513
Ada commits the team's hook to the repository:

82
00:05:16,067 --> 00:05:18,716
…and enables it for herself:

83
00:05:20,467 --> 00:05:22,102
Let me try something.

84
00:05:22,102 --> 00:05:26,386
Grace pulls the hooks, but her commits are not checked:

85
00:05:26,933 --> 00:05:31,329
The hook files arrived with git pull, but Git does not run them:

86
00:05:31,329 --> 00:05:36,481
core.hooksPath is configuration of Ada's clone, not part of the repository.

87
00:05:36,481 --> 00:05:44,518
Git never enables hooks automatically after a clone or pull (that would let any repository run code on your machine).

88
00:05:46,267 --> 00:05:49,415
Grace enables the hooks (in real projects:

89
00:05:49,415 --> 00:05:54,886
a documented one-time setup step), then fixes her message before pushing:

90
00:05:56,633 --> 00:05:57,347
Got it.

91
00:05:57,347 --> 00:06:00,098
What do I need to remember?

92
00:06:00,633 --> 00:06:06,603
Hooks are not cloned; share them as files in the repository plus core.hooksPath.

93
00:06:07,167 --> 00:06:09,879
Git never activates hooks by itself:

94
00:06:09,879 --> 00:06:12,366
a setup step is needed per clone.

95
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:18,504
Local hooks give fast feedback; CI with branch protection gives enforcement.
